Polymorphism is a core object-oriented programming concept that allows objects of different classes to be treated as objects of a common superclass, enabling a single interface to represent different underlying forms. It matters for careers and study because it allows you to write flexible, reusable code that can handle new data types without modifying existing logic; for example, a `PaymentProcessor` class can handle `CreditCard` and `PayPal` objects interchangeably if they both implement a common `process()` method.

```java
// Polymorphism example in Java
interface Payment {
    void process();
}

class CreditCard implements Payment {
    public void process() {
        System.out.println("Processing credit card payment.");
    }
}

class PayPal implements Payment {
    public void process() {
        System.out.println("Processing PayPal payment.");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Payment myPayment = new CreditCard();
        myPayment.process(); // Output: Processing credit card payment.

        myPayment = new PayPal();
        myPayment.process(); // Output: Processing PayPal payment.
    }
}
```
